WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.555
- If Jack McCoy said it
then it's probably true.

00:00:03.555 --> 00:00:06.138
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.320
Hey Legal Eagles, D.
James Stone here teaching

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.850
you how to think like a lawyer

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.654
so you can crush law school.

00:00:13.654 --> 00:00:16.600
When I was growing up, my
parents only let me watch

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.840
a very small amount of TV.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.150
However, they let me watch

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.450
an unlimited amount of educational TV.

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:25.420
So once I decided that
I wanted to be a lawyer

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.060
when I grew up, I convinced
them that legal TV shows

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.680
like Law and Order were educational

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.970
because they would help me
with my future practice.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:38.153
So I grew up watching lots
and lots of Law and Order.

00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:40.070
But to this day, I still like

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.249
watching legal procedurals on TV.

00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:44.550
Last time around, I analyzed a few

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.020
of my favorite courtroom dramas on TV,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.410
this time around, I am reacting to some

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.060
of the TV shows that you
suggested that I watch.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.134
And if you have other suggestions,

00:00:53.134 --> 00:00:54.695
please let me know in the comments,

00:00:54.695 --> 00:00:57.494
I'll try to get to them,
including by popular demand,

00:00:57.494 --> 00:01:00.950
an entire episode about Suits.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.550
But, without further ado, let's get

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.770
to the first drama, L.A. Law.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.270
- Fergus and Associates at the time

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.770
of your discharge, Mr. Cowen?

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:08.684
- Six years.

00:01:08.684 --> 00:01:10.900
Oh boy (whoops).

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.823
- [Lawyer] Why did you
just make that sound?

00:01:12.823 --> 00:01:14.589
- I have Tourette's syndrome.

00:01:14.589 --> 00:01:16.801
- Would you tell the court what that is?

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:19.286
- It's a neurological disorder.

00:01:19.286 --> 00:01:22.470
It causes me to make sounds or say words

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.040
or twitch in different ways.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.558
Sometimes I do it more,
sometimes I do it less.

00:01:27.558 --> 00:01:28.860
- [Lawyer] Can you control it?

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.173
- No, not for any length of time.

00:01:32.306 --> 00:01:35.288
- What reason were you given
for being discharged, Noah?

00:01:35.288 --> 00:01:37.587
- They said I was disruptive.

00:01:37.587 --> 00:01:38.851
- [Lawyer] Were you disruptive?

00:01:38.851 --> 00:01:42.060
- I do market research
and the office I work in,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.020
oh boy, is in the back, and there are only

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.973
a few coworkers around to hear me.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.614
- So far, I really like the tone

00:01:50.614 --> 00:01:53.821
that the attorney is
using with this witness.

00:01:53.821 --> 00:01:57.960
He's conducting what's
called a direct examination.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.616
So, it's his witness, and
he's asking the questions

00:02:00.616 --> 00:02:03.170
that he wants of his own
witness, and he's trying

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.267
to lay the factual foundation for why

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:10.267
this particular individual
was let go for health reasons,

00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:14.690
as opposed to some other pretense reason.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.790
And the questions he's
using are a great example

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.030
of what you're supposed to
do on direct examination.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.020
He's asking open-ended questions.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.458
He's asking, where do you work?

00:02:26.458 --> 00:02:28.808
Were you disruptive?

00:02:28.808 --> 00:02:33.287
The kind of questions that
are like, what happens next?

00:02:33.287 --> 00:02:36.700
Those are the classic
direct examination questions

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.331
that you're not leading the witness,

00:02:38.331 --> 00:02:40.390
you're simply allowing the witness

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.880
to tell the story, and
you're just simply asking

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.440
the next question in a
sequence that allows him

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.530
to go down the line of facts
that you need to illicit.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:50.180
So, so far, the attorney's doing

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.090
a great job on direct examination.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.580
It's a little unrealistic
that he's literally

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.308
putting his hands on the
wall in front of the jury,

00:02:57.308 --> 00:03:00.740
not allowed to do that, but so far,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.240
the content of the questions is very good.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.510
- Did they consider you disruptive?

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.190
- I don't believe so, no.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.088
Nigger, nigger!

00:03:07.088 --> 00:03:10.420
(people murmuring)

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.720
- Why'd you say that, sir?

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.500
- It's all part of Tourette's.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.280
- Noah, would you tell the court

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.590
what it's like to have Tourette's?

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.640
- [Noah] What it's like?

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:20.473
- Yeah.

00:03:21.585 --> 00:03:25.615
- So that might sound like
unnecessarily emotional,

00:03:25.615 --> 00:03:29.362
woo woo question, what is it
like to have Tourette syndrome?

00:03:29.362 --> 00:03:32.037
But that is a great way to build a bond

00:03:32.037 --> 00:03:34.780
between your witness and the jury.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.780
It might not be strictly necessary,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.266
but it adds color to the story,

00:03:39.266 --> 00:03:43.131
and creating a rapport
between your witness

00:03:43.131 --> 00:03:45.630
and garnering sympathy in a way

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.450
that is probative of the question at hand,

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:52.316
specifically, was he let
go for health reasons

00:03:52.316 --> 00:03:54.960
or for some pretextual reason,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.127
is really important in a case like this.

00:03:57.127 --> 00:03:59.946
So that's just a textbook good example

00:03:59.946 --> 00:04:02.598
of a good direct examination

00:04:02.598 --> 00:04:04.983
in an employment case like this.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.920
- Well, the tics and
grimaces start pretty soon

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.330
after you wake up in the morning, oh boy.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.508
And then you go about your
business when you're alone,

00:04:16.508 --> 00:04:19.710
hollering and spitting, barking.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.919
Oh boy, bitch!

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:25.381
It's pretty weird if you're
not used to it, I guess.

00:04:25.381 --> 00:04:30.360
Oh boy, anyway Tourette's is part of me

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.280
and there's nothing I can do about that.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.610
About that, whoop! (sighs)

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.510
- Nothing further.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.563
- Nothing you can do about that?

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:45.670
Tell me, Mr. Cowen, have you ever heard

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.410
of the drug haloperidol?

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:49.270
- Of course I've heard of it.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:50.950
- [Female Attorney] How
about the drug clonidine?

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:53.740
- I prefer not to take either of them.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:54.573
- Why?

00:04:54.573 --> 00:04:55.500
They're both frequently used

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.060
in the treatment of Tourette's.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.660
- Haloperidol, it makes me forget things.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.520
It makes me feel totally spaced out.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.859
And clonidine, it doesn't
work too well with me.

00:05:07.859 --> 00:05:10.457
Whore, slut, bitch!

00:05:10.457 --> 00:05:13.051
- Tell me, Mr. Cowen, did you ever call

00:05:13.051 --> 00:05:15.745
any of your female coworkers bitch?

00:05:15.745 --> 00:05:19.140
- I wasn't calling them
bitch, I was saying bitch.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.140
- How do they know the difference, sir?

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.200
- If they know me, they
know the difference.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.480
- So the burden of your behavior

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.890
should be on them, not on you?

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:26.723
- Objection.

00:05:26.723 --> 00:05:28.460
- Burden, bitch!

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.044
Let me tell you about burden.

00:05:30.044 --> 00:05:34.033
A burden is not being able
to go into a restaurant

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:38.107
or a movie, a burden is (sighs),

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.310
is not being able to
find a woman to love you

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:44.910
or an employer to hire you.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.950
- Mr. Cowen, your attorney
has made an objection.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.790
- A burden is being the kid in class

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:52.410
who everyone point at,
the nut, the weirdo.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:53.243
- [Judge] Mr. Cowen.

00:05:53.243 --> 00:05:54.760
- I am the guy with the
funny disease, all right?

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.710
That's all it is, so stop pointing at me.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:00.673
And stop treating me like I am crazy.

00:06:00.673 --> 00:06:04.033
Oh boy, oh boy, just stop it!

00:06:05.153 --> 00:06:08.726
- The attorney on cross examination
made the classic mistake

00:06:08.726 --> 00:06:13.650
of asking a question that she
doesn't know the answer to.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.240
The first set of questions that she has

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.290
about his medication, she
knows how he's going to answer,

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.990
that he either has tried them in the past

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:26.340
and refuses to use them, or
that there's some other reason

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:27.978
why he will not use the medication,

00:06:27.978 --> 00:06:29.927
and that's part of her case.

00:06:29.927 --> 00:06:32.603
However, she then went beyond that

00:06:32.603 --> 00:06:35.950
to ask an open-ended question about,

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.480
and try to make a legal point
about where the burden lies.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.860
Now, that is not a
question for the witness.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.600
That's a question for
the judge to determine

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.210
whether or not there is a legal basis

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.620
for this employee

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.700
to use medication or not,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.010
and the judge would
solve that legal question

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.177
before the question
gets posed to the jury.

00:06:58.177 --> 00:07:00.540
However, because she has opened the door,

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.316
and because she can't close that door,

00:07:02.316 --> 00:07:05.061
she allows this witness to go off

00:07:05.061 --> 00:07:07.510
on this narrative about the burden

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:11.230
that he faces everyday as
a result of his ailment.

00:07:12.523 --> 00:07:16.530
So, that's exactly how you
let a cross examination

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.650
go off the rails and that's
why you make the points

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.170
that you need to make, in
this case, about medication,

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:26.383
and you stop, and then you
make those argumentative points

00:07:26.383 --> 00:07:30.014
in your closing argument
at the end of the case.

00:07:30.014 --> 00:07:33.304
And when the witness doesn't
have an opportunity to go off

00:07:33.304 --> 00:07:37.984
and again, show his despair
in front of the jury.

00:07:37.984 --> 00:07:39.607
Bad move on cross.

00:07:39.607 --> 00:07:43.313
All right, the next clip
comes from Law and Order.

00:07:45.647 --> 00:07:48.397
(pills clacking)

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.660
- That's a lot of pills, isn't it?

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:00.845
Precisely the amount that Gregory Loomis

00:08:00.845 --> 00:08:03.523
took over his two years on centinol.

00:08:04.753 --> 00:08:07.194
- This appears to be a closing argument

00:08:07.194 --> 00:08:10.392
in a case that involves pharmaceuticals.

00:08:10.392 --> 00:08:13.200
And one of the hardest
things to do as an attorney

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.280
when you're giving a closing argument is,

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:17.640
since you're dealing with
these abstract subjects,

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.166
it's so hard to create
these visual metaphors

00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:23.200
that will resonate with the jury

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.926
because so much of the trial
is just dry facts and figures,

00:08:27.926 --> 00:08:30.480
and I'm sure at some point in this case,

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.000
it came out that this
individual consumed X number

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.260
of pills, but by taking all of those pills

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:41.950
or representatives of
those pills in a pitcher

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:45.172
and then pouring them into another beaker,

00:08:45.172 --> 00:08:49.520
you just get this striking visual metaphor

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.145
that this jury is not going to forget.

00:08:52.145 --> 00:08:56.050
So, that's the kind of thing
that a good trial attorney

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:59.020
will do to come up with these things

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:00.670
that will stick with the jury's mind,

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:02.930
so that even if they forget the facts,

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.372
or forget other portions of the law,

00:09:05.372 --> 00:09:08.885
they will remember that
person pouring thousands

00:09:08.885 --> 00:09:11.980
of pills into a beaker,
that's gonna stick with them.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.990
So that's A-plus lawyering right there.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:16.361
- It was supposed to help him study

00:09:16.361 --> 00:09:19.100
and achieve his full potential.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.264
But what he didn't know, and
what his parents didn't know,

00:09:22.264 --> 00:09:24.263
and what the doctor didn't know

00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:25.980
was that every one of these pills

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:29.233
could be as lethal as a bullet.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.105
No one knew because the
manufacturing drug company

00:09:33.105 --> 00:09:36.079
never told anyone about
possible side effects,

00:09:36.079 --> 00:09:39.471
of suicide, or murder.

00:09:39.471 --> 00:09:44.415
Tragically, one of these bullets went off.

00:09:44.415 --> 00:09:45.900
- That's really great.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:49.530
So, what she's done is,
this is a case involving

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:53.690
a warning label, which can be very dry.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:57.020
You're dealing with this prose that's fit

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:02.020
on to warning label of a
canister of medication.

00:10:02.133 --> 00:10:05.360
And yet, the only thing
that this jury is going

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:09.450
to be thinking about is
this metaphor of a beaker

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.580
of bullets, each one that can go off.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.950
And just this mountain of
medicine that's in front of them.

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:19.270
I mean, that's a great
way to really bring home

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.600
the facts and to create
something that's gonna stick

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.920
with this jury, so well
done by this attorney

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.600
in this closing argument.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:28.103
- And killed Alex Garcia.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.833
Now, Gregory is not a criminal.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.480
He didn't volunteer to take these pills,

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:39.713
it was not his choice, he's a child.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.880
And he did as his parents told him.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.352
And his parents did what
the doctor told them.

00:10:47.352 --> 00:10:50.202
And the doctor prescribed this medication

00:10:50.202 --> 00:10:53.396
because he had been
assured by the manufacturer

00:10:53.396 --> 00:10:54.975
that it was safe.

00:10:54.975 --> 00:10:58.580
Now, follow that line and you'll find

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:00.490
where the blame lies.

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.993
It does not stop at Gregory.

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:08.883
He is not responsible for this tragedy.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:12.190
- So what's going on here
is really interesting

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:15.484
from my standpoint,
because so often you have

00:11:15.484 --> 00:11:17.814
an individual who takes an act,

00:11:17.814 --> 00:11:22.204
but the plaintiffs are
not going to go after

00:11:22.204 --> 00:11:25.040
that individual because often

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.820
that individual doesn't have any money.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:30.380
And so, if you're trying
to get compensation

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:32.659
for some injury that has happened,

00:11:32.659 --> 00:11:37.192
often what you have to do is
to go after the bigger pockets.

00:11:37.192 --> 00:11:42.192
And in many jurisdictions, even if the,

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:45.740
say the manufacturer
of the pharmaceuticals

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:48.180
or the manufacturer of some product

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.467
that was purchased is only
a fraction responsible,

00:11:52.467 --> 00:11:56.634
they can be responsible
for 100% of the damages.

00:11:56.634 --> 00:12:00.682
And even if that party
is not 100% responsible,

00:12:00.682 --> 00:12:03.750
in a lot of jurisdictions, you are allowed

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:06.030
to get 100% of your compensation from

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:07.353
any one of those defendants

00:12:07.353 --> 00:12:09.610
and then the defendant is entitled

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:11.030
to what's called indemnity,

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:13.962
where they're allowed to recoup the rest

00:12:13.962 --> 00:12:17.390
of the damages from the other defendants.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:20.254
So, say a company was
only 10% responsible,

00:12:20.254 --> 00:12:23.377
they might have to pay the plaintiff 100%

00:12:23.377 --> 00:12:26.900
and then go after that
90% from the other people.

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:30.143
And if those people don't have any money,

00:12:30.143 --> 00:12:33.510
that's what's call being judgment-proof,

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:36.034
which is that no one's gonna go after you

00:12:36.034 --> 00:12:38.910
because you'll simply declare bankruptcy.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:41.110
So, it's a real problem in America

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:44.273
where you have people
going after the big pockets

00:12:44.273 --> 00:12:47.405
who are potentially not 100% responsible,

00:12:47.405 --> 00:12:50.300
and it's also a big problem that if you

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:52.382
have the person who is mainly responsible,

00:12:52.382 --> 00:12:56.306
who doesn't have the funds
to compensate the victim,

00:12:56.306 --> 00:12:59.950
then the victim may not
receive compensation.

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:04.780
It's a big problem, that's
why you often have insurance,

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:08.730
is to bridge that gap,
but that's a real issue

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:11.543
that people run into in the legal system.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.440
- No doubt about it, Rebecca Shane

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:24.223
and her team have put
on a dazzling defense.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.740
To hear her tell it,
you could almost think

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:31.063
that no one is responsible for
what happened to Alex Garcia.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:35.340
We're counting on your good sense

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:39.880
to remember that behind
the impressive display,

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:42.019
the facts remain the facts.

00:13:42.019 --> 00:13:46.969
Greg Loomis is an uncommonly
privileged young man

00:13:46.969 --> 00:13:51.969
with a history of violent
reactions to not getting his way.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:55.240
His family's wealth has
allowed him to escape

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:58.970
all the more unpleasant
consequences of his past explosions.

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:00.936
They placed him in new schools,

00:14:00.936 --> 00:14:04.490
and gave him money to avoid hard work,

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.490
and to buy his way out of tough spots.

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:11.490
So, based on past experience, he expects

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:15.085
to evade responsibility
for Alex Garcia's murder

00:14:15.085 --> 00:14:17.930
and to get another free pass.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:19.906
This time from you.

00:14:19.906 --> 00:14:24.906
Who did this, centinol or Greg Loomis?

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:29.970
And how did centinol give Greg Loomis

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.760
his pattern of violent behavior,

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.550
his motive, and the sense of entitlement

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.840
that made him think he
could wrap a garrote

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:41.203
around Alex Garcia's throat
and get away with it?

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.100
- Oh boy, okay, so this
is a criminal prosecution

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:50.100
and apparently the
defendants are using the

00:14:50.231 --> 00:14:53.190
argument of an intervening cause.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.011
And this harkens back to a debate

00:14:55.011 --> 00:14:57.660
that lawyers and philosophers have had

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:59.692
for centuries, if not millennia, which is,

00:14:59.692 --> 00:15:04.020
who's responsible when
there's more than one cause?

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.250
You know, an individual is responsible

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.360
for their own actions, but
there can be intervening

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.250
causes as well, and that's
what the defense is saying.

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:15.590
They're saying that but for the drugs

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:18.180
that this individual was
consuming, he would not

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.090
have conducted these particular acts,

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:23.150
and therefore that should be a defense.

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:27.510
And this debate goes on
in the law all the time.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:30.820
There is a debate between what's
called the proximate cause

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:32.313
and what is the legal cause.

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:36.936
There can be more than one
reason why something happens

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:40.870
and it's a hard decision
for the law to make,

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:42.870
to ultimately say who's responsible

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:44.400
and in what proportion.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:47.623
So, law students deal with
these questions all the time.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.619
- How do we survive as a society

00:15:51.619 --> 00:15:55.460
if no one is accountable
for their actions?

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:56.293
We don't.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:01.863
No one here is willing
to take responsibility.

00:16:03.398 --> 00:16:05.453
So the task falls on you.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:11.410
The law says he must be held
accountable for his actions.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:14.193
Uphold the law.

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:17.823
There isn't an excuse for everything.

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:23.403
- Now, in real life,
the jury would not just

00:16:23.403 --> 00:16:27.320
come up with whatever rule is responsible

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:28.884
in apportioning the blame here,

00:16:28.884 --> 00:16:33.249
the judge will debate that
with the other attorneys

00:16:33.249 --> 00:16:35.595
and they will come up
with jury instructions.

00:16:35.595 --> 00:16:38.543
Both sides will be
responsible for writing down

00:16:38.543 --> 00:16:41.610
the instructions that
they want the jury to get

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:44.603
from the judge, and the
judge will then choose a set

00:16:44.603 --> 00:16:46.700
or write their own, and then give

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:48.780
those instructions to the jury.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:53.680
And you have to give
very clear instructions

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:57.550
so that the jury knows what
standard they're applying.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:16:59.960
Because the jurors are
just like you and me,

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:03.750
they're laypeople, who are not familiar

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:05.740
with the mountains of

00:17:07.798 --> 00:17:09.940
of legal jurisprudence that are out there.

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:11.780
So they're going to have to be given

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:14.500
very specific instructions and this debate

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:16.960
about who is ultimately responsible,

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:19.118
there will be a very specific instruction

00:17:19.118 --> 00:17:22.670
about whether it is proper to hold

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:25.560
the individual responsible or whether

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:27.293
there can be an intervening cause.

00:17:29.174 --> 00:17:31.924
(dramatic music)

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:47.980
Dramatic music.

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:50.810
- In the matter of The
People v. Gregory Loomis,

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:53.280
on the count of murder
in the second degree,

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:54.123
how do you find?

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:58.163
- We find the defendant,
Gregory Loomis, guilty.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:02.583
- [Judge] The jury is dismissed

00:18:02.583 --> 00:18:04.830
with the thanks of the court.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:06.530
- Man, they left out
the jury instructions.

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:08.210
I really would have liked to have seen

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:10.370
what the instructions
the judge gave the jury

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:12.593
so that they could make that decision.

00:18:13.439 --> 00:18:16.189
(dramatic music)

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.880
Jack McCoy said that the law requires them

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:29.890
to find that individual guilty.

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:33.570
So if Jack McCoy said it,
then it's probably true.

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:37.656
All right, the next scene
comes from the TV show Suits.

00:18:37.656 --> 00:18:39.760
- Mr. Evans, is it true that you

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:41.990
were living with Mr. Ross
in and around the time

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:43.830
he claims to have attended Harvard?

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:44.910
- Yes, we shared an apartment

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:47.470
at 5307 Huntington Boulevard in Brooklyn.

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:49.990
- And how exactly did he pay his rent?

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:51.160
- He worked as a bike messenger

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.606
and made money on the side
taking the LSAT's for people.

00:18:53.606 --> 00:18:55.372
- Oh wait, I'm confused.

00:18:55.372 --> 00:18:57.600
Why would he have to resort to that

00:18:57.600 --> 00:18:59.455
if he were a college graduate?

00:18:59.455 --> 00:19:01.180
- Because he wasn't a college graduate.

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:05.140
- What, you mean he never
graduated from college?

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:06.540
- No, he was kicked out of college

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:08.071
for cheating on a test.

00:19:08.071 --> 00:19:10.520
- Unclear how this witness would know

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.640
that information, he could
be relying on hearsay

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:15.250
or he could be relying on things

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.260
that are outside of
his personal knowledge.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.076
I think a good objection
here would have been

00:19:19.076 --> 00:19:21.815
either lack of foundation,
that he hasn't shown

00:19:21.815 --> 00:19:23.950
how he knows this information,

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:26.900
or an objection to speculation

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:29.640
or lack of personal
knowledge if he has no basis

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:31.633
for knowing that information.

00:19:32.844 --> 00:19:37.844
- Well, isn't it possible that
while he was living with--

00:19:38.523 --> 00:19:41.661
- And this attorney
makes the classic TV move

00:19:41.661 --> 00:19:45.292
of walking right into
the well of the court.

00:19:45.292 --> 00:19:48.848
The area between counsel
table and the judge.

00:19:48.848 --> 00:19:52.360
The reason that there are bailiffs is

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:55.246
to protect the judge
and possibly the jury,

00:19:55.246 --> 00:19:58.640
and if you walk into the well unannounced,

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:01.380
you are going to get
tackled by that bailiff.

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:04.715
You have to ask permission
to enter that space

00:20:04.715 --> 00:20:06.260
for the protection of the jury,

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:07.850
and for the protection of the judge.

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:10.020
It happens on TV all the time.

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:11.829
It never happens in real life.

00:20:11.829 --> 00:20:13.420
Also, there's usually a bunch

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:15.364
of clutter and stuff in that area.

00:20:15.364 --> 00:20:18.960
But, do not make the mistake of walking

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.300
into the well of the
court without permission,

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:24.776
that is a recipe for
being found in contempt

00:20:24.776 --> 00:20:27.080
or pissing off the
judge, and you don't want

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:28.280
to do either of those things.

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:30.810
- Living with you, he went back to school

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.533
and completed his degree?

00:20:32.533 --> 00:20:35.160
- Well, I don't see how since
most of his spare time's

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.250
spent sitting around smoking weed with me.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:38.764
- Well let me get this straight.

00:20:38.764 --> 00:20:42.000
Before he was employed at Pearson Spector,

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.580
to the best of your knowledge,

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:45.111
he worked as a bike messenger

00:20:45.111 --> 00:20:48.742
and a part-time LSAT imposter?

00:20:48.742 --> 00:20:52.870
- Apart from the attorney
putting her elbow

00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:55.201
on the witness stand, it's a good question

00:20:55.201 --> 00:20:59.051
to narrow things to his
personal recollection

00:20:59.051 --> 00:21:02.630
and only testifying to
things that he observed

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:05.797
in the presence of the
defendant in this case.

00:21:05.797 --> 00:21:08.156
There should have been some objections.

00:21:08.156 --> 00:21:11.950
You want to break up the
flow of the other side

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:13.773
if they're making some really good points.

00:21:13.773 --> 00:21:18.760
And also, you may prevent them
from getting certain evidence

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:21.180
into the court if you're able to show

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:23.440
that they haven't met
the evidentiary burden.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.669
In this case, personal
knowledge or foundation.

00:21:25.669 --> 00:21:29.165
You don't want to let
the other side go off

00:21:29.165 --> 00:21:31.670
and get momentum in their questioning,

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:34.016
especially when they're
making big points against you.

00:21:34.016 --> 00:21:35.483
- [Mr. Evans] Yes.

00:21:35.483 --> 00:21:37.813
- One last question Mr. Evans.

00:21:37.813 --> 00:21:40.734
Is it at all possible that Mike Ross

00:21:40.734 --> 00:21:44.093
ever attended Harvard Law School?

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:48.170
- Not unless they have a satellite campus

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:50.713
at 5307 Hunting Boulevard in Brooklyn.

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:54.390
- Unclear that this witness would know

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:58.141
what kind of time constraints
are on law students

00:21:58.141 --> 00:22:00.610
or how he could know whether anyone

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:02.791
could possibly go to law school.

00:22:02.791 --> 00:22:05.770
I mean, he doesn't have
any knowledge about

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:08.270
what the schedule of a law student is.

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:10.760
So that, again, would
be a good opportunity

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:12.810
to object and to prevent that testimony

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:15.760
from coming in because it
doesn't sound proper to me

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:18.270
based on that foundation.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.860
All right, so those are some
of my favorite legal TV shows.

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:23.540
If you enjoyed this video
and would like to see

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:26.020
more lawyer reacts videos or hear about

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:27.130
my law school experience,

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:29.189
click on this short playlist
that I put together.

00:22:29.189 --> 00:22:31.050
It includes my reaction to some

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.930
of the most famous legal
scenes in the movies.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:35.430
And please subscribe so you

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:37.030
don't miss out on the next video.

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:39.335
Otherwise, I'll see you in the next one.

00:22:39.335 --> 00:22:40.685
(upbeat music)

